WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.608
Sign in once, and use many applications.

00:00:03.608 --> 00:00:08.143
With the OpenID Connect module, your Orchard Core site can be the identity

00:00:08.143 --> 00:00:10.184
provider that signs people in.

00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:15.060
This video explains how it works, and shows where its features are in the admin.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.626
A company may run a website, a mobile app and a partner portal.

00:00:23.626 --> 00:00:27.994
If each one keeps its own accounts and passwords, people sign in again

00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:31.730
and again, and you must manage access in every application.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.656
An identity provider fixes this.

00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:37.347
It is the one place where people sign in.

00:00:37.347 --> 00:00:42.272
Each application sends people there, and receives tokens that say who the person

00:00:42.272 --> 00:00:44.620
is and what the application may do.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:50.679
The OpenID Connect module turns your Orchard Core site into that identity provider.

00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:55.929
People sign in with their Orchard Core user accounts, and applications that follow

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:59.270
the OpenID Connect and OAuth standards can use it.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.463
Here is how a sign-in works.

00:01:05.463 --> 00:01:10.620
An application, such as a mobile app, sends the person to Orchard Core to sign in.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.553
If the person is not signed in, Orchard Core shows its login page.

00:01:15.553 --> 00:01:19.968
It can then ask whether to grant the application access, and lists the

00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:24.790
scopes requested, which are named kinds of access, such as the email address.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.776
When the person agrees, Orchard Core sends them back to the application with a code.

00:01:30.776 --> 00:01:35.430
The application exchanges that code for tokens, including an access token.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.922
The application sends the access token with each request to an API.

00:01:40.922 --> 00:01:44.104
The API checks the token before it answers.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:50.340
For the API of your own site, the OpenID Connect Token Validation feature does this check.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.430
Orchard Core can play three roles.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:58.111
It can be the identity provider that issues tokens, the API that validates

00:01:58.111 --> 00:02:02.850
them, or the client that signs people in through another identity provider.

00:02:07.055 --> 00:02:10.781
The module's features are on the Features page, under Tools.

00:02:10.781 --> 00:02:12.475
Let's search for OpenID.

00:02:12.943 --> 00:02:16.804
The results are grouped under the category OpenID Connect.

00:02:16.804 --> 00:02:22.473
The list hides OpenID Connect Core Services, which other features turn on automatically.

00:02:22.938 --> 00:02:28.308
OpenID Connect Authorization Server makes your site an identity provider.

00:02:28.308 --> 00:02:32.318
It signs people in and issues tokens to applications.

00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:36.378
OpenID Connect Client Integration works the other way around.

00:02:36.378 --> 00:02:41.159
It lets people sign in to your site with an account from another identity provider.

00:02:41.609 --> 00:02:47.275
OpenID Connect Management UI adds the pages for managing applications and scopes.

00:02:47.275 --> 00:02:52.549
The authorization server depends on it, so enabling the server turns it on.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:59.282
OpenID Connect Secrets lets the server use signing and encryption keys that are stored as secrets.

00:02:59.282 --> 00:03:01.696
It depends on the Secrets feature.

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:07.568
OpenID Connect Token Validation checks tokens, whether this site issued them or another

00:03:07.568 --> 00:03:12.347
trusted identity provider did, so that the API of your site can accept them.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.432
Let's enable the authorization server.

00:03:19.432 --> 00:03:25.698
Its row lists two dependencies, OpenID Connect Core Services and OpenID Connect Management

00:03:25.698 --> 00:03:28.440
UI, which are enabled along with it.

00:03:28.903 --> 00:03:30.432
Let's click Enable.

00:03:30.432 --> 00:03:36.243
A notification confirms that the feature OpenID Connect Authorization Server was enabled.

00:03:36.697 --> 00:03:42.092
Back on the list, the authorization server and the Management UI show Enabled.

00:03:42.092 --> 00:03:45.111
Core Services is enabled too, but hidden.

00:03:45.111 --> 00:03:50.957
Client Integration, Secrets and Token Validation stay disabled until you enable them.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:58.859
Enabling a feature adds its pages to the admin menu.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:04.268
The server's settings are under Settings, OpenID Connect, Authorization Server.

00:04:04.728 --> 00:04:09.597
A warning at the top says that saving these settings might restart the website.

00:04:09.597 --> 00:04:15.268
The settings follow in groups, starting with Endpoints, the addresses that applications call.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.885
Flows are the ways an application can get tokens.

00:04:18.885 --> 00:04:23.488
By default, the Authorization Code Flow and the Refresh Token Flow are on,

00:04:23.488 --> 00:04:25.560
and every endpoint is enabled.

00:04:26.017 --> 00:04:31.931
Further down are the groups Proof Key for Code Exchange and Pushed Authorization Requests.

00:04:31.931 --> 00:04:37.107
The last group, Advanced Options, is marked as intended for advanced use only.

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:42.219
Applications are under Access Control, OpenID Connect, Applications.

00:04:42.219 --> 00:04:47.309
An application is a program that is registered to ask this site for tokens.

00:04:47.309 --> 00:04:49.948
The list is empty until you add one.

00:04:50.406 --> 00:04:52.388
Scopes are in the same menu.

00:04:52.388 --> 00:04:56.412
A scope names a kind of access that an application can request.

00:04:56.412 --> 00:04:59.386
The list has Email, Phone, Profile and Roles.

00:05:00.986 --> 00:05:05.767
Each feature adds its own pages to the admin menu when it is enabled.

00:05:05.767 --> 00:05:12.970
Under Settings, OpenID Connect, these are Authorization Server, Token Validation and Authentication Client.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:17.496
Applications and Scopes are under Access Control, OpenID Connect.

00:05:19.496 --> 00:05:21.817
That's the OpenID Connect module:

00:05:21.817 --> 00:05:25.745
Orchard Core as the identity provider for your applications.

00:05:25.745 --> 00:05:32.708
Separate videos cover the server settings, applications and scopes, the client, and token validation in detail.

00:05:32.708 --> 00:05:34.256
Thanks for watching.

